AB  - The rapid development of XR (Extended Reality) has made optimizing user experience and commercialization essential for successful technology deployment. This paper examines 505 valid XR applications, analyzing how content attributes and price types influence user reputation. A quantitative approach is applied, using K-means clustering, Welch’s t-test, Pearson correlation, and two-way ANOVA. Results show that historical content ranks highest in user reputation, with educational and social science content performing lower. Content attributes have a significant effect, while price types show a weak yet significant correlation with user reputation ($\mathrm{r}=0.133, \mathrm{p}=0.027$). No significant interaction effect between content and price was found ($p=0.455$). Based on these findings, a three-dimensional optimization framework for XR-focusing on experience, content, and price-is proposed, driving a closed-loop system for continuous improvement and monitoring. This research provides insights for enhancing XR applications’ user experience and system iteration in consumer-facing scenarios.
ER  - 

