AB  - As Social VR platforms continue to expand, ensuring user safety against interpersonal harassment has become an increasingly critical challenge. Existing mitigation approaches—largely dependent on human moderation or invasive biometric sensing—suffer from limited scalability, privacy concerns, and insufficient contextual understanding. In this paper, we present Social VR Administrator (SVRA), a context-aware intelligent agent framework designed to provide cost-effective, transparent, and automated moderation in social VR environments. SVRA employs a novel modular architecture comprising three key components: A Context Analyzer that interprets behavioral norms by incorporating environmental settings and social relationships; a Dynamic Model Selector that adaptively balances detection accuracy and computational efficiency by escalating analysis from lightweight screening models to advanced Vision Language Models (VLMs); and an Action Module that executes graduated interventions ranging from warnings to blocking. Experimental results demonstrate that SVRA effectively balances detection accuracy with resource consumption, enabling a robust, privacy-preserving harassment prevention.
ER  - 

