AB  - This paper investigates user needs and multimodal meeting minutes for an immersive virtual reality (VR) collaboration platform. Although VR extends the possibilities of remote meetings, it still makes note-taking and later review difficult, especially when participants are absent or miss parts of the discussion. To better understand these challenges, we conducted a user needs assessment via a structured questionnaire to characterise current practices, expectations, and perceived issues around meetings, automated documentation, and VR-based summaries. The results indicate a clear preference for concise, action-oriented meeting reports, as well as a strong interest in role-based customization. Guided by these findings, we design a post-hoc workflow that combines speech transcription (Whisper), speaker diarization (pyannote.audio), and a GPT-based large language model to generate role-aware, adaptive meeting reports. The system leverages multimodal data from immersive interactions to produce contextualised minutes.
ER  - 

