AB  - Virtual reality (VR) is increasingly used for training in manufacturing and laboratory contexts, yet repurposing content for new procedures remains bespoke and labor intensive. We sketch an LLM-driven pipeline in which large language models (LLMs) transform expert materials (videos or manuals, with CAD/scans when available) into two artifacts: a structured instruction plan and a companion assessment plan bound to a semantically labeled scene graph. A minimal runtime delivers stepwise guidance with preventive feedback and summarizes performance via rubric-aligned scoring. We share preliminary feasibility observations across several scenarios and an initial usability study indicating promising user experience. The approach aims to reduce authoring effort and lower technical barriers while preserving adaptability across domains.
