AB  - The rapid development of virtual reality (VR) head-sets has enabled users to perform office tasks such as composing emails and documents using virtual keyboards. However, typing on mid-air virtual keyboards presents challenges in terms of efficiency and speed. This paper presents a method that leverages skeleton-based deep learning models applied to hand skeletons, a previously unexplored approach for predicting keystrokes in VR devices. The proposed method first extracts the skeleton of the hands from each frame in the video to form a sequence of skeletons. This sequence of skeletons is processed to determine whether the user is typing and to predict which key is pressed. Finally, a language model is utilized to refine these predictions. The proposed method showed promising results, achieving an accuracy of 94.61% for classifying keystrokes. In keystroke recognition research for VR devices, there are no publicly available datasets. To address this, we curated a dataset named KeyVr, which is now accessible to the research community.
ER  - 

