AB  - Collecting rich qualitative feedback in virtual reality (VR) remains challenging. While standardized questionnaires can assess presence, VR sickness, or user experience using 2D user interfaces (UI) in 3D space, obtaining detailed open-ended responses requires cumbersome text entry, which researchers often avoid. We present and evaluate a novel approach using a large language model (LLM)driven intelligent virtual agent (IVA) that conducts spoken questionnaire interviews in VR. In a within-subjects study ($\mathrm{N}=36$), we compared an IVA interviewer to a conventional in-VR 2D-UI questionnaire, administering both the Igroup Presence Questionnaire (IPQ) and open-ended questions. The results demonstrate that the IVA reliably collects data without systematic measurement distortion in Likert scales, elicits $3.5 \times$ longer and more diverse free-text responses, and reduces workload for open-ended questions while trading off efficiency for closed items. Our findings establish LLM-driven IVAs as a viable and advantageous survey modality for VR research, particularly for collecting rich qualitative feedback that traditional methods struggle to capture. We discuss design implications and provide recommendations for when IVA-based questionnaires offer the greatest benefit.
ER  - 

