AB  - The article presents the results of the development and experimental testing of an intelligent VR simulator designed to improve the effectiveness of practical training for specialists in the field of information-measuring and control systems (IMCS). The relevance of the research is driven by the challenges of modern engineering education: high equipment costs, the inability to simulate non-standard situations, limited access to complex technical systems, and the lack of a personalized learning approach. The proposed solution integrates virtual and augmented reality technologies with artificial intelligence (AI) elements, including computer vision based on YOLOv8 for recognizing industrial automation objects and the Mistral 7B language model operating on the Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) principle for contextual learner support. The scientific novelty lies in the creation of an adaptive educational system capable of analyzing user actions in real time, identifying typical errors, and forming individual learning trajectories. During experimental testing involving USPTU students, the practical effectiveness of the simulator was proven: the time to complete laboratory work decreased by 15.79%, and the number of errors during the assembly of measuring equipment decreased by 31.81% compared to traditional methods. Methodological recommendations for scaling the technology into the curricula of technical universities have been developed. The research results are supported by three certificates of state registration of computer programs.
ER  - 

