AB  - Effective layout of user interfaces (UIs) in immersive virtual reality (VR) environments is critical for information visibility, interaction efficiency, and overall user experience. Traditional evaluation relies on time-consuming expert judgment, while existing automated 2D methods fail to generalize to 3D VR contexts burdened by spatial depth, occlusion, and diverse interaction modalities. To address these limitations, we present a multimodal large language model (LLM)-driven framework for the automated assessment of VR UI layouts, which jointly considers the visual, spatial, and interactive characteristics of the interface. We conducted two complementary studies to validate our approach: a user study in representative VR scenes to examine how our method influences task performance, and an expert validation study involving 51 VR interface layouts (222 annotated violations) to assess the reliability of LLM-based evaluations. Experimental results demonstrate that our method significantly improves task efficiency and performs well in detecting and mitigating issues related to visibility and interactivity. This work offers a practical and scalable solution for evaluating and optimizing VR UI layouts, providing new insights into model-driven interface design in immersive environments.
ER  - 

