AB  - With the widespread application of virtual reality (VR) technology in interactive media, the multimodality and semantic sparsity of massive user behaviour data pose significant challenges for efficient annotation. Addressing the issues of low accuracy in complex interaction scenarios and high manual annotation costs associated with existing methods, this study proposes a fully automated annotation system based on a dualstream hybrid inference architecture. To address the scarcity of high-quality training data in this specialised domain, we first designed a "trajectory-driven multimodal data simulation" method, constructing the VR-Museum-Behaviour dataset that integrates authentic museum collection semantics. At the core algorithmic level, the system innovatively integrates a static rule engine with a semantic inference engine based on a large language model (Qwen3-8B), resolving decision conflicts through a confidence-adaptive fusion mechanism. Experimental results demonstrate that the system achieves an F1 score of 0.7696 on the test set, elevating recall to 1.00, significantly outperforming traditional rule-based baselines (F1=0.3462). Moreover, the system operates at over three times the efficiency of manual annotation while generating fine-grained user profiles aligned with cognitive psychology theories. This research provides a high-quality, lowcost data engineering solution for training immersive intelligent interaction models.
